{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRz4gPknHzrKY3UXPgK4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017QFe8P2pjqTQnJSKE3SZFK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/h-4OGZds && python3 - <<'E'\nimport re\np='server.py'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('''DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL = 3600   # seconds; used when the provider does not say how long a token lives\n''','')\ns=s.replace('''    expires_in = int(tok.get(\"expires_in\") or DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL)\n    refresh = tok.get(\"refresh_token\")\n    if not refresh:\n        log(f\"{user_id}: no refresh_token in response, scheduling expiry in {expires_in}s\")\n''','''    # Intervals.icu tokens are long-lived and come without expires_in/refresh_token;\n    # only schedule an expiry when the provider actually states one.\n    expires_in = int(tok[\"expires_in\"]) if tok.get(\"expires_in\") else None\n    refresh = tok.get(\"refresh_token\")\n''')\ns=s.replace('\"expires_at\": time.time() + expires_in,','\"expires_at\": time.time() + expires_in if expires_in else None,')\ns=s.replace('(expires in {expires_in}s)\")','(expires in {expires_in or \"never\"}s)\")')\ns=s.replace('''c[\"status\"] != \"connected\" or c[\"expires_at\"] - now > 300:''','''c[\"status\"] != \"connected\" or c[\"expires_at\"] is None or c[\"expires_at\"] - now > 300:''')\ns=s.replace('''        self.redirect(AUTHORIZE_URL + \"?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode(params, safe=\":,\"))''','''        url = AUTHORIZE_URL + \"?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode(params, safe=\":,\")\n        if self.headers.get(\"Accept\", \"\").startswith(\"application/json\"):\n            # SPA path: navigation can't carry the Bearer header, so the SPA fetches the URL.\n            return self.send_json(200, {\"url\": url})\n        self.redirect(url)''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='static/app.js'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('<a class=\"btn\" href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">Connect Intervals.icu</a>','<button class=\"btn\" id=\"connect\">Connect Intervals.icu</button>')\ns=s.replace('''    const d = document.getElementById(\"disconnect\");''','''    const c = document.getElementById(\"connect\");\n    if (c) c.onclick = async () => {\n      try { location.href = (await api(\"/api/intervals/auth/init\", { headers: { \"Accept\": \"application/json\" } })).url; }\n      catch (e) { root.insertAdjacentHTML(\"beforeend\", `<p class=\"card\">Error: ${esc(e.message)}</p>`); }\n    };\n    const d = document.getElementById(\"disconnect\");''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='config.py'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('SCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE\"','''# Last set Intervals accepted (Rivera, 2026-09-19). The 2026-10-02 addition of\n# ACTIVITY:READ/CALENDAR:* is what the authorize endpoint rejects with a bare 400.\nSCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ\"''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Causes\n\n1. **Connect button always 401s.** It was a plain `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`. A browser navigation cannot send the `Authorization: Bearer` header (the token lives in localStorage and is only added by the SPA's `fetch` wrapper), so `require_user` rejected everyone. Fix: the button now calls `auth/init` via `api()` with `Accept: application/json`; the server returns `{\"url\": ...}` and the SPA navigates to it. Plain requests still get the 302.\n2. **Authorize step returns a bare 400 (`invalid_request`).** It began when calendar sync (2026-10-02) widened the scopes to `ACTIVITY:READ`, `CALENDAR:READ`, `CALENDAR:WRITE`. Intervals answers 400 without naming the parameter. The only authorize that ever worked was with a narrower scope (the granted scope was `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`). `config.SCOPES` is reverted to that set. **Not verified against Intervals** (no network or docs access, and I did not contact the service). Calendar features need the correct calendar scope names from the Intervals OAuth docs added back, one at a time. If the 400 persists, check `redirect_uri` registration for the client next.\n3. **\"Reconnect\" after an hour.** The token response has no `expires_in` or `refresh_token` (Intervals tokens are long-lived), but the code assumed a 3600s default lifetime and then expired the connection because there was no refresh token. Fix: no expiry is scheduled unless the provider states one; the refresher skips such connections.\n\nNot run end to end against the real Intervals service.\nE\npython3 -m py_compile server.py && node --check static/app.js 2>&1 | head -3"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":265,"cache_read_input_tokens":29482,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":265},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"922dcd20-8d72-439b-a089-ee21fd035c96","uuid":"95d3a9ac-6dde-4a79-a624-1ef320f4a4f2","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:52:44.789Z","request_id":"req_011CgRz4gA7R3npqmL9WkZqv","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017QFe8P2pjqTQnJSKE3SZFK":{"command":"cd /tmp/h-4OGZds && python3 - <<'E'\nimport re\np='server.py'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('''DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL = 3600   # seconds; used when the provider does not say how long a token lives\n''','')\ns=s.replace('''    expires_in = int(tok.get(\"expires_in\") or DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL)\n    refresh = tok.get(\"refresh_token\")\n    if not refresh:\n        log(f\"{user_id}: no refresh_token in response, scheduling expiry in {expires_in}s\")\n''','''    # Intervals.icu tokens are long-lived and come without expires_in/refresh_token;\n    # only schedule an expiry when the provider actually states one.\n    expires_in = int(tok[\"expires_in\"]) if tok.get(\"expires_in\") else None\n    refresh = tok.get(\"refresh_token\")\n''')\ns=s.replace('\"expires_at\": time.time() + expires_in,','\"expires_at\": time.time() + expires_in if expires_in else None,')\ns=s.replace('(expires in {expires_in}s)\")','(expires in {expires_in or \"never\"}s)\")')\ns=s.replace('''c[\"status\"] != \"connected\" or c[\"expires_at\"] - now > 300:''','''c[\"status\"] != \"connected\" or c[\"expires_at\"] is None or c[\"expires_at\"] - now > 300:''')\ns=s.replace('''        self.redirect(AUTHORIZE_URL + \"?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode(params, safe=\":,\"))''','''        url = AUTHORIZE_URL + \"?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode(params, safe=\":,\")\n        if self.headers.get(\"Accept\", \"\").startswith(\"application/json\"):\n            # SPA path: navigation can't carry the Bearer header, so the SPA fetches the URL.\n            return self.send_json(200, {\"url\": url})\n        self.redirect(url)''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='static/app.js'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('<a class=\"btn\" href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">Connect Intervals.icu</a>','<button class=\"btn\" id=\"connect\">Connect Intervals.icu</button>')\ns=s.replace('''    const d = document.getElementById(\"disconnect\");''','''    const c = document.getElementById(\"connect\");\n    if (c) c.onclick = async () => {\n      try { location.href = (await api(\"/api/intervals/auth/init\", { headers: { \"Accept\": \"application/json\" } })).url; }\n      catch (e) { root.insertAdjacentHTML(\"beforeend\", `<p class=\"card\">Error: ${esc(e.message)}</p>`); }\n    };\n    const d = document.getElementById(\"disconnect\");''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='config.py'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('SCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE\"','''# Last set Intervals accepted (Rivera, 2026-09-19). The 2026-10-02 addition of\n# ACTIVITY:READ/CALENDAR:* is what the authorize endpoint rejects with a bare 400.\nSCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ\"''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Causes\n\n1. **Connect button always 401s.** It was a plain `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`. A browser navigation cannot send the `Authorization: Bearer` header (the token lives in localStorage and is only added by the SPA's `fetch` wrapper), so `require_user` rejected everyone. Fix: the button now calls `auth/init` via `api()` with `Accept: application/json`; the server returns `{\"url\": ...}` and the SPA navigates to it. Plain requests still get the 302.\n2. **Authorize step returns a bare 400 (`invalid_request`).** It began when calendar sync (2026-10-02) widened the scopes to `ACTIVITY:READ`, `CALENDAR:READ`, `CALENDAR:WRITE`. Intervals answers 400 without naming the parameter. The only authorize that ever worked was with a narrower scope (the granted scope was `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`). `config.SCOPES` is reverted to that set. **Not verified against Intervals** (no network or docs access, and I did not contact the service). Calendar features need the correct calendar scope names from the Intervals OAuth docs added back, one at a time. If the 400 persists, check `redirect_uri` registration for the client next.\n3. **\"Reconnect\" after an hour.** The token response has no `expires_in` or `refresh_token` (Intervals tokens are long-lived), but the code assumed a 3600s default lifetime and then expired the connection because there was no refresh token. Fix: no expiry is scheduled unless the provider states one; the refresher skips such connections.\n\nNot run end to end against the real Intervals service.\nE\npython3 -m py_compile server.py && node --check static/app.js 2>&1 | head -3"}}}
